

--> Spring Security Basics

    Authentication vs Authorization
        | Concept            | Meaning                          | Example                        |
        | ------------------ | -------------------------------- | ------------------------------ |
        | **Authentication** | Verifies **who you are**         | Login with username & password |
        | **Authorization**  | Verifies **what you can access** | Only ADMIN can delete users    |

    Flow in Spring Security
    👉 Authentication happens first
    👉 Authorization happens after authentication

--> Spring Security Architecture (High Level)
    Spring Security is based on a Servlet Filter Chain.
    Every HTTP request goes through security filters before hitting your controller.
    🔧 Core Components (Easy Explanation)

    | Component                  | Role                                |
    | -------------------------- | ----------------------------------- |
    | **SecurityContext**        | Stores logged-in user details       |
    | **Authentication**         | Represents user credentials & roles |
    | **AuthenticationManager**  | Main entry point for authentication |
    | **AuthenticationProvider** | Validates credentials               |
    | **UserDetailsService**     | Loads user from DB / memory         |
    | **PasswordEncoder**        | Encrypts & verifies passwords       |


--> Filters & Roles
    Security Filters
    Intercept every request
    Handle:
    Authentication
    Authorization
    Exception handling
    🔹 Roles (Authorities)
    Define permissions
    Usually prefixed with ROLE_


